
Think of a Kubernetes cluster as having two major sides:

                    KUBERNETES CLUSTER
                           |
             +-------------+-------------+
             |                           |
      CONTROL PLANE                  WORKER NODES
      "The Manager"                 "Where apps run"
             |                           |
      API Server                     Kubelet
      Scheduler                      Kube-proxy
      Controller Manager             Container Runtime
      etcd                           Pods

The simplest definition:

Control Plane decides what should happen. Worker Nodes actually run the applications.

For example, you tell Kubernetes:

"I want 3 copies of my backend application running."

The control plane figures out where those 3 copies should run.

The worker nodes actually run them.

2. Before Kubernetes: Cluster → Node → Pod → Container

This hierarchy is extremely important.

Kubernetes Cluster
│
├── Control Plane
│
└── Worker Nodes
      │
      ├── Worker Node 1
      │     ├── Pod
      │     │    └── Container
      │     └── Pod
      │          └── Container
      │
      └── Worker Node 2
            └── Pod
                 └── Container

Suppose your application has:

Frontend
Backend
Database

Kubernetes could eventually run:

Worker Node 1
├── frontend-pod
│     └── frontend container
│
└── backend-pod
      └── backend container


Worker Node 2
├── backend-pod
│     └── backend container
│
└── postgres-pod
      └── postgres container

Important:

Kubernetes doesn't normally deploy your source code directly.

It runs containers created from container images.

That's why your pipeline makes sense:

