3. Kubernetes Control Plane

Now let's open up the "brain."

CONTROL PLANE
│
├── API Server
├── etcd
├── Scheduler
└── Controller Manager

Each has a completely different responsibility.

4. API Server — the front door

The kube-apiserver is probably the most important component to understand first.

Almost everything communicates through it.

Imagine you execute:

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

kubectl doesn't directly contact a worker node.

Instead:

You
 |
kubectl
 |
 v
API SERVER
 |
 +---- etcd
 |
 +---- Scheduler
 |
 +---- Controller Manager
 |
 +---- Kubelet

So think:

API Server = front door/receptionist of Kubernetes.

Requests to create, modify, delete, or retrieve Kubernetes resources go through the API server.

For example:

kubectl get pods
kubectl get nodes
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl delete pod backend
kubectl scale deployment backend --replicas=5

All of these interact with the Kubernetes API.

5. etcd — Kubernetes database

Next is etcd.

Think:

etcd = memory/database of the Kubernetes cluster.

It stores Kubernetes cluster state.

For example, Kubernetes needs to know:

What Deployments exist?
What Services exist?
How many replicas are requested?
What ConfigMaps exist?
What Secrets exist?
What Nodes exist?

That information is persisted in etcd.

Suppose your YAML says:

replicas: 3

The desired cluster state includes:

backend deployment
desired replicas = 3

If only two Pods are running, Kubernetes can detect:

Desired = 3
Actual  = 2

and work toward restoring the desired state.

This leads to one of Kubernetes' most important ideas:

Desired State vs Actual State
Desired State
      |
      | Kubernetes continuously reconciles
      v
Actual State

You declare:

"I want 3 backend Pods."

Kubernetes continuously works toward:

3 backend Pods running

If one crashes:

Before

Pod 1 ✅
Pod 2 ✅
Pod 3 ✅

        ↓

Pod 1 ✅
Pod 2 ❌
Pod 3 ✅

        ↓ Kubernetes reconciles

Pod 1 ✅
Pod 3 ✅
New Pod 4 ✅

This is the foundation of Kubernetes self-healing.

6. Scheduler — decides WHERE a Pod runs

Suppose Kubernetes needs to create a new backend Pod.

Your cluster has:

Worker Node 1
Worker Node 2
Worker Node 3

Who decides which node gets the Pod?

kube-scheduler.

New Pod needs to run
        ↓
     Scheduler
        ↓
Looks at available nodes
        ↓
CPU / Memory
Constraints
Taints / Tolerations
Affinity rules
etc.
        ↓
Selects Node 2

So:

Scheduler = decides which Worker Node should run a Pod.

Important distinction:

The scheduler doesn't run the container itself.

It only makes the placement decision.

7. Controller Manager — continuously checks the state

This is where Kubernetes starts becoming very interesting for SRE.

Think:

Controller Manager = continuously watches whether reality matches what you requested.

Example:

Desired replicas = 3
Actual replicas  = 2

Controller detects:

Something is wrong.

Need one more Pod.

Then Kubernetes works toward creating another Pod.

There are multiple controllers handling different resources and lifecycle tasks.

Conceptually:

              Desired State
                   |
                   v
          Controller Manager
                   |
             compare state
                   |
          +--------+--------+
          |                 |
       matches           doesn't match
          |                 |
      do nothing       take corrective
                          action

This is called a reconciliation loop.

You'll hear this term frequently in Kubernetes.

8. Now move to Worker Nodes

Control Plane:

API Server
etcd
Scheduler
Controller Manager

Worker:

WORKER NODE
│
├── Kubelet
├── Container Runtime
├── Kube-proxy
│
└── Pods
      └── Containers

Let's understand each.

9. Kubelet — manager/agent on each Worker Node

Every worker node runs a kubelet.

Think:

Kubelet = Kubernetes agent responsible for Pods on that particular node.

Suppose scheduler decides:

backend-pod → Worker Node 2

The kubelet on Worker Node 2 sees that the Pod is assigned there and works with the container runtime to get the containers running.

Conceptually:

API Server
     |
     | Pod assigned to Node 2
     v
Kubelet
     |
     v
Container Runtime
     |
     v
Pull Image
     |
     v
Create Container
     |
     v
Pod Running

The kubelet also reports Pod/node status back through the Kubernetes API.

So if you run:

kubectl get pods

and see:

backend-123   1/1   Running

that state is part of Kubernetes' continuously updated cluster information.

10. Container Runtime

Kubernetes itself isn't the low-level component that executes containers.

Worker nodes need a container runtime, commonly containerd or CRI-O.

Conceptually:

Kubelet
   ↓
Container Runtime
   ↓
Container Image
   ↓
Running Container

For example:

Docker Hub

nikhilbecomessre/backend:v5
          ↓
      Worker Node
          ↓
      containerd
          ↓
       Container

One important interview point:

Modern Kubernetes uses the CRI (Container Runtime Interface) to work with compatible runtimes such as containerd and CRI-O. Docker Engine itself is no longer directly integrated through the old dockershim.

11. Pod — smallest deployable unit

This is one of the most important concepts.

Kubernetes generally doesn't say:

Run my container.

The deployable abstraction is:

POD
 └── Container

So:

Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.

Most commonly:

1 Pod
  ↓
1 main application container

Example:

backend-pod
      |
      └── Java container

But a Pod can contain multiple containers when they need to operate very closely together:

Pod
├── Main Application
└── Sidecar

Containers inside the same Pod share networking and can share volumes.

12. kube-proxy — service networking

Now suppose you have:

frontend-pod
backend-pod

Frontend needs to reach backend.

But Pods are replaceable.

Today:

backend-pod IP = 10.244.1.5

Pod crashes.

Replacement might be:

backend-pod IP = 10.244.2.8

You don't want your frontend hardcoding changing Pod IP addresses.

That's where Services come in.

Frontend
    |
    v
Backend Service
    |
    +------> Backend Pod 1
    |
    +------> Backend Pod 2
    |
    +------> Backend Pod 3

kube-proxy traditionally implements node-level Service traffic rules, although some modern Kubernetes networking solutions can implement Service routing differently.

For learning purposes:

Service provides stable access to Pods; kube-proxy commonly helps implement that traffic routing.

